+3D cases (2026-10-28). A 3D mesh has no `empty` patch, so the cell polygons
+come from a plane slice instead (`PythonSlice.H`): OpenFOAM's `cuttingPlane`
+at z = `LLM_SLICE_Z` (default: the middle of the mesh's z range), normal
+(0 0 1), not triangulated, so each cut cell gives one polygon, its section. A
+plane through mesh points (the middle of an even number of uniform layers)
+would lie on the faces between two cells; it is moved halfway to the next
+point plane above. The polygons go through the same three arguments, with the
+same per-rank cell offsets and gather, and an eighth argument describes the
+plane; a 2D case gets the seven-argument call as before. v2 pictures then
+draw the slice cells only, each as its own polygon in its own colour, and the
+v2 frame holds only those cells: `fields.npz` keeps their centres (the 3D
+cell centres), U and p, plus `mesh_cells`, `polygon_points` and
+`polygon_offsets`; `readable-metadata.json` gets `slice` (z, requested z,
+where it came from, whether it was moved, cells in the slice and in the mesh).
+The request is built as for 2D; neither the pictures nor the request say that
+they show a slice. An unreadable `LLM_SLICE_Z` or a plane that cuts no cells
+leaves the solver running and v2 requests refused with the reason. v1
+pictures of a 3D case keep the nearest-cell raster of all cells. Tested on
+the step case extruded to 8 cells in z, serially and on 2 ranks
+([evidence](../../../docs/evidence/2026-10-28-3d/README.md)); not tested on
+other 3D meshes or with a model.
